1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,900
 All right, welcome to week three.

2
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:05,440
 You might notice I'm wearing my Ferrari jumper again.

3
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:08,840
 This has been filmed the day after the Hungarian GP

4
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:10,360
 and we had a marginally better week.

5
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:13,240
 So I don't feel too bad about wearing red this week.

6
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:17,520
 This week we are going to focus on rotating mechanical systems.

7
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:20,960
 These are like the big brother of what we did last week.

8
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:23,200
 Last week we looked at things as point masses, right?

9
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,400
 We said they can only translate.

10
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:27,480
 Well, this week we're going to be looking at things

11
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:29,960
 as rigid bodies, kind of the next step up

12
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:33,000
 in terms of modeling them as close to reality.

13
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,560
 When we're dealing with rigid bodies,

14
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:37,400
 size and shape actually begin to matter.

15
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:39,960
 In contrast to last week where we could kind of treat

16
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:41,800
 everything as a point and it didn't matter

17
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:45,360
 if it was shaped to like a point or shaped like a potato,

18
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:47,000
 this week if something shaped like a potato,

19
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
 it does matter because the size and the shape

20
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:51,920
 have a profound impact on the way that things rotate.

21
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:53,400
 This week we're going to look at things

22
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:56,800
 which only rotate and we'll specifically only rotate

23
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:00,600
 about one axis in the axial rotation.

24
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,280
 Maybe later in the course we'll look at trying

25
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:04,720
 to mix up things which both translate and rotate

26
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:07,400
 but for this week for what we're getting started

27
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:09,840
 we'll stick to just rotating about one axis.

28
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:12,000
 So what about states?

29
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,760
 At the end of last week I promised you that our state selection

30
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:17,160
 procedure was going to get a whole lot easier

31
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:19,280
 because we understood kind of what was going on

32
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:20,120
 underneath the hood.

33
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:22,680
 We took a step back and we looked at the big picture

34
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:25,480
 and I remember saying to you, okay, in general

35
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:28,720
 we're going to choose energy variables as states.

36
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:30,880
 So rather than introduce you to the two variables

37
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:32,760
 we'll choose as states instead

38
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:35,000
 I'm going to introduce you to the two energy variables

39
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,800
 for the rotating mechanical system domain.

40
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:40,800
 And those energy variables are angular momentum

41
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:42,720
 and angular displacement.

42
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:44,640
 And if you're thinking to yourselves, hold on,

43
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:47,040
 the energy variables for translating mechanical systems

44
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:49,640
 were linear momentum and linear displacement.

45
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:52,720
 This is basically the same thing, you would be right.

46
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:55,040
 I promised you that it'd be all sorts of beautiful analogs

47
00:01:55,080 --> 00:01:58,360
 between the different domains and this is the very first one.

48
00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:00,440
 The energy variables for translating systems

49
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:02,920
 and rotating systems are almost identical.

50
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:06,360
 This is the angular analog of what we met last week.

51
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:08,760
 So whenever we see angular momentum

52
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:10,880
 or we see angular displacement,

53
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:13,000
 those are the things we're going to choose as states.

54
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:14,080
 What's even nicer?

55
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:17,320
 The same rule of thumb that we had for translating mechanical

56
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:19,960
 systems actually applies in terms of how many states

57
00:02:19,960 --> 00:02:21,920
 we need for rotating systems.

58
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:24,240
 We are once again going to want two states

59
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:27,000
 for every degree of freedom in the system.

60
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,320
 Now when things are rotating,

61
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:29,720
 the concept of degrees of freedom

62
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:31,960
 is pretty much the same as it was before.

63
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:34,560
 Every mass that can rotate about its own axis

64
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:36,720
 that's disconnected from other masses.

65
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:38,440
 It's going to be treated as independent

66
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:41,160
 or I think I used the word disconnected last week.

67
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:44,200
 And that mass will be said to have its own degree of freedom.

68
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:47,320
 So basically, as long as two masses aren't sharing a shaft

69
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:49,120
 aren't traveling at the same speed,

70
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:52,000
 we'll consider them to be independent or disconnected.

71
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:53,880
 And they'll have a degree of freedom of their own.

72
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:55,920
 All right, in the same way as last week,

73
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:57,960
 let's go and meet some fundamental components

74
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:00,000
 and then we'll do some more of my problems.
